  "id": "COMPOUND:30360 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between sec-Butyl acetate, (-)- and Phosphodiesterase 6C, as it is identified as a target of multiple compounds that are themselves targets of SNU-387, a compound that is also a target of Genipin 1-O-Beta-D-Isomaltoside and 3-Bromo-4-(2,3-Dibromo-4,5-Dihydroxybenzyl)-5-Methoxymethylpyrocatecol. These compounds are all implicated in the regulation of phosphodiesterase 6C, a key enzyme involved in the breakdown of cyclic nucleotides, which plays a role in cellular signaling and homeostasis. The shared target of these compounds with Phosphodiesterase 6C implies a possible mechanism of action involving the inhibition of this enzyme, which could be a central pathway in the therapeutic effects of sec-Butyl acetate, (-)-. The evidence is supported by the presence of multiple independent pathways leading to the same target, suggesting a plausible and consistent mechanism of action. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that sec-Butyl acetate, (-)- may act through a mechanism that involves the modulation of phosphodiesterase 6C, though further experimental validation is required to confirm this relationship.",
